<div class="content">
    <h1>Teaching guide</h1>
    <p class="content__intro">You do not need training to teach these lessons. You need to read the script, watch the child, and follow a few rules about pace, correction, and mastery. This guide explains those rules and the reasons for them.</p>

    <div class="guide-section">
      <h2 class="guide-section__title">How the program is built</h2>
      <p>Math Direct follows the design of Siegfried Engelmann's Direct Instruction programs. Its starting belief is simple: if a child has not learned, the teaching was not yet clear enough. So every step is written to leave as little room for misunderstanding as possible.</p>
      <p>There are three levels, <a href="/prek/">Pre-K</a>, <a href="/k/">Kindergarten</a>, and <a href="/first/">First Grade</a>, and an optional <a href="/bridge/">Bridge to Second Grade</a>. Each level is a list of skill tracks: counting, reading numbers, shapes, adding, and so on. A track runs across many lessons. Each lesson takes short exercises from several tracks, so a child might count, read numbers, add, and name shapes in the same ten minutes.</p>
      <p>When a step is new, it is taught in full. For the next two lessons it is firmed. After that it returns for review at widening intervals and never drops away for long. This spacing is what makes learning last. Every ten lessons a <strong>mastery test</strong> checks each skill taught. The <a href="/curriculum/">scope and sequence</a> lists every step and the lesson where it begins.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-section">
      <h2 class="guide-section__title">Before you start</h2>
      <ul class="guide-tips">
        <li><strong>Find the starting lesson.</strong> Give the <a href="/placement/">placement test</a>, or begin at lesson 1 of the level that fits the child's age. If the first lessons are too easy, move ahead five lessons at a time.</li>
        <li><strong>Add each child.</strong> Tap Add under Child on the Today screen. Records are kept for each child separately. Check the name before you begin, so results go to the right child.</li>
        <li><strong>Gather what you need.</strong> Each level lists the real objects it uses, such as ten small objects to count. You need nothing else.</li>
        <li><strong>Set up the screen.</strong> On a tablet or computer, sit beside the child. The child looks at the large picture; you read the panel. On a phone held upright, the child's picture is on top and your panel is below.</li>
        <li><strong>Plan the time.</strong> One lesson a day, four or five days a week. Pre-K lessons take about ten minutes, Kindergarten fifteen to twenty, and First Grade twenty to thirty.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-section">
      <h2 class="guide-section__title">The lesson screen</h2>
      <p>The large area is the child's screen. It shows only what the child needs to see: numbers, dots, pictures, shapes. It never shows words for the child to read. The panel is yours. For each step it shows:</p>
      <div class="guide-cards">
        <div class="guide-card"><h3 class="guide-card__title">The cue</h3><p><strong>My turn</strong>: you show the answer. <strong>Together</strong>: you and the child answer together. <strong>Your turn</strong>: the child answers alone, and you mark it. <strong>Set up</strong>: an instruction for you, such as laying out objects.</p></div>
        <div class="guide-card"><h3 class="guide-card__title">What to say</h3><p>The words to read aloud, exactly as written. The wording is chosen so that every example means the same thing.</p></div>
        <div class="guide-card"><h3 class="guide-card__title">Do</h3><p>What to do with your hands or voice: point, pause, touch each picture, hold a number long.</p></div>
        <div class="guide-card"><h3 class="guide-card__title">Child</h3><p>The answer the child should give. A response in parentheses, such as "(Touches 7.)", is an action rather than words.</p></div>
        <div class="guide-card"><h3 class="guide-card__title">If right</h3><p>A short confirmation to say, such as "Yes, seven."</p></div>
        <div class="guide-card"><h3 class="guide-card__title">If wrong</h3><p>The correction for this exact item. Before you mark the answer, it shows in full when there is room; on a small screen a short note stands in its place. When you press Error, the whole correction appears in red in place of the confirmation.</p></div>
      </div>
      <p>The line at the top names the child, the level, the lesson, and the exercise. The thin rail under it shows the exercises: filled when done, hatched where there was an error. The controls sit at the bottom, in four places that never move. <strong>More</strong> opens a menu: back one step, start the exercise over, the outline of the lesson with the objects each exercise needs, a big picture, the keys, switching child, and leaving. The second place holds <strong>Show again</strong>, which brings back dots that were shown briefly, or <strong>Clear marks</strong>, which removes the rings a child makes when touching pictures to count them. <strong>Error</strong> is third. The main button is always last, under your thumb: <strong>Next</strong>, <strong>Firm</strong>, or <strong>Go on</strong>.</p>
      <p>Every mark is saved as you make it. If you leave in the middle of a lesson, or the phone rings and the screen closes, the Today screen offers to continue where you stopped, for up to twelve hours. Finishing the lesson clears the saved sitting.</p>
      <p>When the child touches an answer on the screen, the touched picture gets a ring and the panel tells you what was touched and whether it is right. The screen never shows the child a verdict. You still mark the answer: press Firm or Error as you judge it. Show again lets the child try once more before you mark.</p>
      <p>The lesson screen is built to fit on one screen without scrolling. When a step has a lot of text, the panel hides the exercise details and makes the type a little smaller until everything fits. Long counting answers are shown to you as numerals, such as 105, 106, 107, so that a skipped number is easy to spot; you still say them as words.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-section">
      <h2 class="guide-section__title">How to present a step</h2>
      <ul class="guide-tips">
        <li><strong>Read the words as written</strong>, in a clear, ordinary voice. Do not add explanations. Extra talk makes a step less clear, not more.</li>
        <li><strong>Use a signal.</strong> When the script says "Get ready," pause about a second, then nod or touch the screen. The child answers on the signal. This gives every child thinking time and stops guessing.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep a steady pace.</strong> A brisk pace holds attention. Move on as soon as an answer is firm.</li>
        <li><strong>Watch, not just listen.</strong> When a child counts objects, watch that each touch goes with one number. When a child touches a picture to answer, watch that it is the first touch, not a second try.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-section">
      <h2 class="guide-section__title">Marking Firm and Error</h2>
      <p>On every "Your turn" step, tap <strong>Firm</strong> or <strong>Error</strong>. When the child touches a picture to answer, the program marks it for you; you can change the mark.</p>
      <p>Mark <strong>Firm</strong> only for an answer that is right, given on the signal, and given without help. Mark <strong>Error</strong> for a wrong answer, and also for one that is late, that sounds like a guess, that needed a hint, or that was only partly right. This is strict on purpose. A skill is firm when the child is sure of it, and a later lesson will build on it.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-section">
      <h2 class="guide-section__title">Correcting an error</h2>
      <div class="guide-feedback-row">
        <div class="guide-feedback guide-feedback--correct">
          <h3>The correction</h3>
          <p><strong>Model</strong> the answer ("My turn: seven"), <strong>lead</strong> ("Say it with me: seven"), and <strong>test</strong> again ("Your turn. How many?"). The panel shows the words for each item.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-feedback guide-feedback--praise">
          <h3>Then start over</h3>
          <p>After correcting, press <strong>Start exercise over</strong> (on a phone, <strong>Start over</strong>) to go back to the beginning of that exercise. Doing the whole exercise again shows whether the child can now do it among the other items, not only right after hearing the answer.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>Every item the child missed comes back at the end of the lesson as a delayed test. If it is still missed then, the summary tells you which exercise to reteach at the start of the next lesson.</p>
      <p>Correct calmly and without comment. An error is information about the teaching, not a fault in the child.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-section">
      <h2 class="guide-section__title">After the lesson</h2>
      <p>When the last step is done, you see a summary for yourself, not for the child. It gives the number of firm answers, the errors and where they were, and one piece of advice:</p>
      <ul class="guide-tips">
        <li><strong>Go on</strong> when everything was firm by the end.</li>
        <li><strong>Go on, but reteach one exercise first</strong> when an item was still missed at the end.</li>
        <li><strong>Repeat the lesson</strong> when more than one answer in four needed correcting, or when several items were still missed at the end. Repeating is normal and costs little. Pushing ahead on a weak step costs much more later.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The advice is a guide, not a rule. Under it, <strong>Mark this lesson</strong> lets you record the lesson as finished or to repeat, whatever the advice said.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-section">
      <h2 class="guide-section__title">Changing a mark yourself</h2>
      <p>You can set any lesson's mark by hand: for instance, when a lesson was taught away from the screen, when the wrong child was selected, or when you want to go back over an earlier lesson. Open the level's page, choose <strong>Change marks</strong>, and tap the lesson. Then choose one of these:</p>
      <ul class="guide-tips">
        <li><strong>Mark finished</strong> records the lesson as done. Marking a later lesson finished moves the child on to the lesson after it.</li>
        <li><strong>Mark to repeat</strong> puts the lesson on the Records screen's list of lessons to repeat.</li>
        <li><strong>Reset to not started</strong> erases the lesson's record for that child, so it shows as never taught.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Mastery tests can be marked the same way: passed, to give again, or not given. Choose <strong>Done</strong> when you have finished. Teaching a lesson again replaces any mark you set by hand with the new result.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-section">
      <h2 class="guide-section__title">Mastery tests</h2>
      <p>A mastery test follows every tenth lesson. It has one short part for each skill taught in those ten lessons; the last test of a level covers the whole level. Give the test as written, <strong>without correcting or helping</strong>. Mark each answer and go on. The panel hides the corrections during a test.</p>
      <p>A part is passed with no errors, or with one error when it has four or more items. For any part not passed, the results list the exact lessons and exercises to reteach, with links. Reteach those, give that part again, and go on when it is passed.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-section">
      <h2 class="guide-section__title">Practice and fact review</h2>
      <p><a href="/practice/">Practice</a> gives ten items from any one skill track, drawn only from lessons the child has finished. Use it on a day without a lesson, or for a skill that keeps showing errors. The <a href="/facts/">fact review</a> drills the addition and subtraction facts taught so far, with missed facts coming up first. A fact counts as firm after three right answers in a row. The fact chart on the Records screen shows where each fact stands.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-section">
      <h2 class="guide-section__title">Praise</h2>
      <p>Praise briefly, and name what was done: "Yes, seven." "You started at the top." "You stopped right on twelve." Specific praise tells the child what was right, so it teaches. General excitement teaches nothing and can make an answer feel like a performance. That is why the program has no points, stickers, or celebrations.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-section">
      <h2 class="guide-section__title">When a child struggles</h2>
      <ul class="guide-tips">
        <li>Repeat the lesson the next day. Most difficulty clears on the second teaching.</li>
        <li>If the same skill fails twice, look at its track on the level page and reteach the lesson where that step began.</li>
        <li>Check "Where the errors are" on the Records screen. Errors that cluster in one skill point to the step to reteach.</li>
        <li>Shorten the session rather than skipping exercises. Stop on a success.</li>
        <li>If many skills are weak, give the placement test again. Starting a little earlier is no loss.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-section">
      <h2 class="guide-section__title">Records</h2>
      <p>Lesson results, test results, fact records, the placement result, and your notes are saved for each child in this browser. Nothing is sent anywhere. Use <strong>Save a backup</strong> on the <a href="/records/">Records</a> screen now and then, and <strong>Load a backup</strong> to carry records to another device. Clearing the browser's data for this site erases the records.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-section">
      <h2 class="guide-section__title">Keyboard shortcuts</h2>
      <p>With a keyboard attached, these keys work during lessons, tests, and practice:</p>
      <div class="shortcut-grid">
        <div class="shortcut-item"><kbd>&#8594;</kbd> or <kbd>Enter</kbd><span>Next step. On a "Your turn" step, mark Firm and go on.</span></div>
        <div class="shortcut-item"><kbd>F</kbd><span>Mark Firm</span></div>
        <div class="shortcut-item"><kbd>X</kbd> or <kbd>E</kbd><span>Mark Error and show the correction</span></div>
        <div class="shortcut-item"><kbd>&#8592;</kbd><span>Go back one step</span></div>
        <div class="shortcut-item"><kbd>S</kbd><span>Start the exercise over</span></div>
        <div class="shortcut-item"><kbd>Space</kbd><span>Show again: bring back the dots shown briefly</span></div>
        <div class="shortcut-item"><kbd>C</kbd><span>Clear marks</span></div>
        <div class="shortcut-item"><kbd>O</kbd><span>Outline</span></div>
        <div class="shortcut-item"><kbd>B</kbd><span>Big picture</span></div>
        <div class="shortcut-item"><kbd>M</kbd><span>More</span></div>
        <div class="shortcut-item"><kbd>?</kbd><span>The list of keys</span></div>
        <div class="shortcut-item"><kbd>Esc</kbd><span>Close a sheet, or leave the lesson</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

